title: LSP overlay diagnoses gitignored files (routes by path prefix, not snapshot membership)
updated_at: "2026-08-24T03:12:19Z"
---
Found by the B015 lane (GH #60) and independently by its codex reviewer,
2026-08-24. Deliberately scoped OUT of B015, which covers discovery only
(B015 decision 4).

## Symptom
With `honor_gitignore` enabled (the new default), a gitignored `.as` file is no
longer part of the snapshot's file set — but opening that file in an editor still
produces diagnostics. `Backend::on_change` routes an open buffer to a workspace
by PATH PREFIX, not by file-set membership, so the buffer is checked against a
snapshot that does not contain its gitignored siblings' declarations.

## Why it matters
The failure mode is NEW false positives, which is the failure mode this repo
cares about most: a plugin that gitignores compile-critical sources (dev-loop
codegen output is the motivating case in GH #60) gets undefined-identifier /
unknown-type squiggles on the very file the author opened, because its siblings
are invisible to the snapshot. CLI `check` and the editor now disagree about
what is being compiled.

## Escape hatch that exists today
`honor_gitignore = false` (or `--no-gitignore` for the CLI) restores the old
behaviour on both paths, so nobody is stuck — but the default configuration is
internally inconsistent, and the inconsistency is silent.

## Candidate resolutions (decide, don't assume)
1. **Route by membership**: if the opened buffer is not in the snapshot's file
   set, do not publish diagnostics for it at all (publish an empty set), so the
   editor matches `check`. Simple, and arguably the honest reading of "this file
   is excluded".
2. **Publish one informational diagnostic** on line 1 saying the file is excluded
   by `.gitignore` and naming the escape hatch, instead of a body of bogus
   errors. More discoverable, more intrusive.
3. **Include the opened buffer in its own snapshot** as an overlay member even
   when gitignored, so it type-checks against the real workspace. Keeps
   diagnostics useful, but re-introduces exactly the squiggles-on-an-excluded-
   file surprise GH #60 set out to remove.
Recommend 1 with a `tracing::info!` naming the file and the escape hatch, then
revisit if a user asks for 3.

## Work
- Decide the resolution above; the choice is a UX decision, not a mechanical fix.
- Make `Backend::on_change` (and the didOpen/didSave paths) consult snapshot
  membership rather than path prefix.
- Check the same question for `compute_diagnostics` single-file mode.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: a server test that opens a gitignored `.as` file in a workspace whose
`.gitignore` excludes it, and asserts the chosen behaviour (no diagnostics under
resolution 1) — fails today, which is the point. Variants: gitignored file whose
siblings define the symbols it uses (the FP case); `honor_gitignore = false`
restores full diagnostics; `--no-gitignore`-equivalent server setting; a NON
gitignored file in the same workspace still diagnoses normally (guard against
over-suppression); didSave and didChange both behave the same.

## Provenance
tests/gitignore_server_tests.rs (added by B015) pins the discovery half of this
on the server path; the overlay half is what remains.

RESOLVED by B020 / GH #61, merged as c4bc77f (2026-08-24). Implemented via this task's own recommended resolution 1: a usage-only buffer publishes an EMPTY diagnostic set from both publishers (publish_sharded_diagnostics and publish_legacy_diagnostics) across didOpen/didChange/didSave, so the editor agrees with check. Both remainders landed in the same branch rather than as a further follow-up: the INFO log naming the file and the escape hatches, and a named test (omitted_buffer_using_sibling_declarations_is_not_diagnosed) pinning the motivating FP - a gitignored file using a sibling class, member and function - whose second half is the non-vacuity control asserting a CHECKED file still reports the undefined symbol. Work item 3 (compute_diagnostics single-file mode) audited and found moot: that function is now reachable only via compute_manifest_diagnostics and two integration tests; no live .as route goes through it.